---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';

const sections = [
  { id: 'context', label: 'Context' },
  { id: 'tenant', label: 'Tenant administration' },
  { id: 'identity', label: 'Identity & access' },
  { id: 'support', label: 'Support practice' },
  { id: 'reflection', label: 'What I take forward' },
];
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Microsoft 365 & Identity Administration — Pratik Deshmukh"
  description="My personal Microsoft 365 Developer sandbox: tenant settings, Entra ID users and groups, role-based access and simulated helpdesk troubleshooting."
  canonicalPath="/work/microsoft-365-identity/"
  sections={sections}
>
  <Fragment slot="hero">
    <span class="eyebrow case-project-label">02 / PERSONAL ADMINISTRATION SANDBOX</span>
    <h1 id="case-title">
      Identity is an
      <br />
      <span class="accent">operational responsibility.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>
      Practising Microsoft 365 tenant administration, Entra ID and access management in a personal
      Developer environment.
    </p>
    <div class="case-meta">
      <div>
        <small class="muted">ENVIRONMENT</small>
        <b>Microsoft 365 Developer sandbox</b>
      </div>
      <div>
        <small class="muted">FOCUS</small>
        <b>Tenant settings, users & roles</b>
      </div>
      <div>
        <small class="muted">SCOPE</small>
        <b>Personal practice / simulated support</b>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
  <div class="split" slot="figure">
    <figure class="drawing">
      <span class="mono">MICROSOFT 365 / ADMINISTRATION WORKFLOW</span>
      <div class="identity-flow">
        <div class="identity-step">
          <small>01</small>
          <b>Users & groups</b>
          <span>Provision</span>
        </div>
        <div class="identity-step">
          <small>02</small>
          <b>Roles & access</b>
          <span>Assign</span>
        </div>
        <div class="identity-step">
          <small>03</small>
          <b>Support scenarios</b>
          <span>Investigate</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        Personal Developer environment. An overview of the practice, without tenant or user
        information.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">THE ADMINISTRATION WORKFLOW</span>
      <h2>
        Provision.
        <br />
        Assign.
        <br />
        Investigate.
      </h2>
      <p class="workflow-intro">
        Work through the relationship between identities, administrative roles and the questions
        that reach a support queue.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section id="context" aria-labelledby="context-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">01 / CONTEXT</span>
    <h2 id="context-title">
      A place to practise
      <br />
      administrative decisions.
    </h2>
    <p>
      I managed a personal Microsoft 365 Developer environment as a Global Administrator to practise
      tenant administration and identity workflows. The sandbox gave me a place to configure
      settings, provision identities and navigate the administrative tools.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is personal project experience. My professional role at Teleperformance was in
      international non-voice customer care; the sandbox is where I developed hands-on familiarity
      with Microsoft 365 administration.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="tenant" aria-labelledby="tenant-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">02 / TENANT ADMINISTRATION</span>
    <h2 id="tenant-title">
      Understand the
      <br />
      scope of a change.
    </h2>
    <p>
      I worked with tenant-wide settings, domains and security policies in the Microsoft 365 admin
      tools. The focus was understanding the administrative environment and how to find the controls
      relevant to a task.
    </p>
    <p>
      A tenant setting and a user-specific issue operate at different scopes. Making that
      distinction is a useful starting point for administration and troubleshooting, especially when
      a broad privilege makes many changes possible.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="identity" aria-labelledby="identity-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">03 / IDENTITY & ACCESS</span>
    <h2 id="identity-title">
      Users, groups
      <br />
      and role boundaries.
    </h2>
    <p>
      I provisioned users and groups through Microsoft Entra ID and implemented role-based access
      control. Working with these objects made identity and access management a practical
      administrative task rather than just a list of concepts.
    </p>
    <div class="drawing">
      <span class="mono accent">THREE QUESTIONS ABOUT ACCESS</span>
      <div class="identity-flow">
        <div class="identity-step">
          <small>01</small>
          <b>Identity</b>
          <span>Who?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="identity-step">
          <small>02</small>
          <b>Role assignment</b>
          <span>What access?</span>
        </div>
        <div class="identity-step">
          <small>03</small>
          <b>Administrative scope</b>
          <span>Where?</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="diagram-note">
        A conceptual way to reason about permission, without exposing individual assignments.
      </p>
    </div>
    <h3>Distinguish privilege from the task</h3>
    <p>
      Global Administrator was the access level in my practice environment. Having that privilege is
      different from demonstrating a least-privilege design. The useful question is which
      permissions a particular task requires, and at what scope.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="support" aria-labelledby="support-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">04 / SUPPORT PRACTICE</span>
    <h2 id="support-title">
      Follow the question
      <br />
      through the admin tools.
    </h2>
    <p>
      I used the Microsoft 365 admin center to simulate common corporate helpdesk escalations. The
      work connected administrative navigation with the habit of understanding a question before
      choosing a change.
    </p>
    <p>
      My customer support experience brings a complementary discipline: follow the process,
      communicate clearly and coordinate an escalation when another team needs to be involved. The
      sandbox let me apply that way of thinking while learning tenant and identity tools.
    </p>
    <p>
      These were practice scenarios, separate from customer tickets or administration of an
      employer’s Microsoft 365 tenant.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="reflection" aria-labelledby="reflection-title">
    <span class="eyebrow">05 / WHAT I TAKE FORWARD</span>
    <h2 id="reflection-title">
      Administration meets
      <br />
      support experience.
    </h2>
    <p>
      The project connects user and group management, tenant settings, access roles and support
      workflows. It adds hands-on administration practice to my background in handling customer
      questions and escalations.
    </p>
    <p>
      More detailed scenario notes would make the work easier to revisit: the initial symptom, the
      scope inspected, the action taken and the result. That is the next step in turning practical
      familiarity into a clearer troubleshooting record.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href="/#contact">
      Get in touch ↗
    </a>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>

<style>
  .case-project-label {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 40px;
  }
  .workflow-intro {
    margin-top: 24px;
  }
  .diagram-note {
    margin-top: 20px;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }
</style>
